00:00:18.125 --> 00:00:21.000
But if I may, I'd like to pull you
back to the beginning of your career.

00:00:21.000 --> 00:00:22.900
You started off in
the automobile industry.

00:00:22.900 --> 00:00:24.320
What prompted the move to tech?

00:00:25.580 --> 00:00:28.410
&gt;&gt; Well, I listened to this
journalist describing that

00:00:28.410 --> 00:00:29.620
he said how many years, right?

00:00:31.130 --> 00:00:33.780
You are all looking at me
thinking I look older or younger?

00:00:33.780 --> 00:00:38.390
[LAUGH] And also we were just
outside talking about this.

00:00:38.390 --> 00:00:40.280
You've had Mary Barra, too, right?

00:00:40.280 --> 00:00:43.322
So, because I started
actually at General Motors.

00:00:43.322 --> 00:00:47.063
And so why did I move?

00:00:47.063 --> 00:00:50.810
I have to tell you something and
it's kind of an interesting story.

00:00:52.320 --> 00:00:55.080
General Motors had at the time
back when I went to university,

00:00:55.080 --> 00:00:58.730
and I went to Northwestern and
I was putting myself through school.

00:00:58.730 --> 00:01:02.790
And back in those days GM offered me
a scholarship, no strings attached,

00:01:02.790 --> 00:01:04.300
nobody does that anymore.

00:01:04.300 --> 00:01:07.970
But no strings attached
helped me with school

00:01:07.970 --> 00:01:10.010
if I would just work in the summers.

00:01:10.010 --> 00:01:14.446
And I felt a great obligation then to try.

00:01:14.446 --> 00:01:16.014
But what I always say to people,

00:01:16.014 --> 00:01:19.208
what I learned at what I thought
was a really young age at the time,

00:01:19.208 --> 00:01:22.307
is that I learned the importance
of passion about what you did.

00:01:22.307 --> 00:01:25.587
And I said to Mary, you love cars.

00:01:25.587 --> 00:01:28.130
I like helping you, but I wasn't in love.

00:01:28.130 --> 00:01:30.130
In fact, I was working on trucks and
buses, okay?

00:01:30.130 --> 00:01:32.420
And I'm like, I wasn't in love.

00:01:32.420 --> 00:01:37.420
And it taught me something and I probably
didn't crystallize it till later in life

00:01:37.420 --> 00:01:40.440
that it was the importance of being
passionate about what you do.

00:01:40.440 --> 00:01:43.579
And I guess that's so
easy to say and especially for

00:01:43.579 --> 00:01:48.463
everybody here who's you're doing
something now you like or you will do.

00:01:48.463 --> 00:01:50.580
Boy, life is too short.

00:01:50.580 --> 00:01:54.210
And so you've got to keep moving
til you find what that is.

00:01:54.210 --> 00:01:57.200
And I'm not even sure I knew
what it was at the time.

00:01:57.200 --> 00:01:59.530
I could only see what it wasn't.

00:01:59.530 --> 00:02:01.890
And so that was what made me move.

00:02:01.890 --> 00:02:05.750
And so because I said I love technology,
because I was an engineer.

00:02:05.750 --> 00:02:09.300
I loved it, but
I wanted to apply it to do things.

00:02:09.300 --> 00:02:12.030
And that's what lead me then back to it.

00:02:12.030 --> 00:02:13.223
And actually it wasn't much.

00:02:13.223 --> 00:02:15.900
I wish I could tell you it was
a really complex job search.

00:02:17.220 --> 00:02:19.510
I had looked,
I had had a lot of job offers, but

00:02:19.510 --> 00:02:23.900
at the time I was getting married and
my husband was from the midwest.

00:02:23.900 --> 00:02:27.470
And so I know for all the job
offers I had on the West Coast,

00:02:27.470 --> 00:02:30.040
they couldn't understand
why was I not leaving.

00:02:30.040 --> 00:02:34.419
The cold the snow of Chicago until I
finally admitted I was getting married,

00:02:34.419 --> 00:02:36.001
and I was moving over here.

00:02:36.001 --> 00:02:39.872
And I ended up there for as I thought a
lot about it I said, where could I go that

00:02:39.872 --> 00:02:44.180
I could use technology in any industry and
that's what led me to IBM.

00:02:44.180 --> 00:02:48.550
So passion was my first lesson
at a really young age to learn.

00:02:48.550 --> 00:02:49.600
&gt;&gt; Well, passion clearly worked.

00:02:49.600 --> 00:02:52.469
You're now the CEO of one of the most
iconic companies in the world.

00:02:52.469 --> 00:02:54.266
To those of us sitting in these seats,

00:02:54.266 --> 00:02:57.710
it can occasionally be daunting to
think about the journey to being CEO.

00:02:57.710 --> 00:03:00.954
Can you tell us whether you were ever
intentional about taking that job, and

00:03:00.954 --> 00:03:03.447
what kind of the challenges
might have been along the way?

00:03:03.447 --> 00:03:05.900
&gt;&gt; Well, let me do a poll, so
this is somewhat interactive, all right?

00:03:05.900 --> 00:03:10.265
So how many people think they
want to be CEO of anything?

00:03:10.265 --> 00:03:12.470
Okay so half, half.

00:03:12.470 --> 00:03:13.370
How many are not sure?

00:03:14.780 --> 00:03:17.890
The other half, or
the other third, so to speak.

00:03:17.890 --> 00:03:22.345
because I was talking to Jordi about this,
I said you know what?.

00:03:22.345 --> 00:03:23.910
There was never a goal.

00:03:23.910 --> 00:03:26.240
It was never ever a goal for me.

00:03:26.240 --> 00:03:29.320
And I think there's a lesson in that,
because I think there's been a lot of

00:03:29.320 --> 00:03:32.750
people who've always said,
this has gotta be a mission.

00:03:32.750 --> 00:03:34.562
You've gotta want to do this.

00:03:34.562 --> 00:03:37.910
People who want this job stake it out and
go for it.

00:03:37.910 --> 00:03:40.200
And I don't prescribe to that school.

00:03:40.200 --> 00:03:46.590
I prescribe to the school that you just
keep doing what it is you love to do,

00:03:46.590 --> 00:03:49.850
you do it great, it opens a door,
you do something else.

00:03:49.850 --> 00:03:52.860
And that sort of led to this place, right?

00:03:52.860 --> 00:03:54.830
So I can't say I ever started that way.

00:03:54.830 --> 00:03:59.601
I know people want me to say that, but
I can't say that it was really that way.

00:03:59.601 --> 00:04:03.404
But I think the other thing, if I can, not
that if you have it as a question or not.

00:04:03.404 --> 00:04:06.618
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; But the other thing what you made me

00:04:06.618 --> 00:04:12.156
think of though is, again,
if I can share something of value on this.

00:04:12.156 --> 00:04:15.103
I do think people often say to me and,
John,

00:04:15.103 --> 00:04:19.740
when you introduced me you said
I'd been at IBM now it's 37 years.

00:04:19.740 --> 00:04:21.288
And that may seem like a long time, well,

00:04:21.288 --> 00:04:23.378
it's obviously double most of your ages or
something.

00:04:23.378 --> 00:04:28.093
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; But so why did I stay, right?

00:04:28.093 --> 00:04:29.560
People will say why did you stay?

00:04:30.820 --> 00:04:34.990
And in addition to being passionate,
I feel really strongly,

00:04:34.990 --> 00:04:38.370
whatever you go do,
do something with purpose.

00:04:38.370 --> 00:04:41.700
You can define what that means,
but do something with purpose.

00:04:41.700 --> 00:04:45.160
And I always felt to this
day in different ways and

00:04:45.160 --> 00:04:47.420
I'll come back to me even now as CEO.

00:04:47.420 --> 00:04:51.150
But is that IBM had
a purpose in this world

00:04:51.150 --> 00:04:54.430
in that this world would be
different if it wasn't for IBM.

00:04:54.430 --> 00:04:56.917
And it doesn't mean everything
we do is that way, and

00:04:56.917 --> 00:05:00.198
there will be clearly other great
companies and you'll go off and do.

00:05:00.198 --> 00:05:03.085
But there are so
many things I can point back in time, and

00:05:03.085 --> 00:05:07.708
forward to, and say wouldn't have existed
without, and that's this idea of purpose.

00:05:07.708 --> 00:05:12.544
And so I started, John said as an
engineer, but I've done engineering, I've

00:05:12.544 --> 00:05:18.339
done sales, I've done research, I've done
finance, I feel like I've had 100 careers.

00:05:18.339 --> 00:05:23.594
I did a consulting company, I did
a technical company, all within one place.

00:05:23.594 --> 00:05:28.130
So that ability to work from
anything you can dream of, and

00:05:28.130 --> 00:05:31.938
then in those wildest moments,
it has purpose.

00:05:31.938 --> 00:05:37.131
And I was thinking at the day,
a client I had met about seven years ago,

00:05:37.131 --> 00:05:41.534
we had started some work together,
you mentioned Watson,

00:05:41.534 --> 00:05:43.852
and we started in healthcare.

00:05:43.852 --> 00:05:46.874
Healthcare is one of the most difficult.

00:05:46.874 --> 00:05:47.900
Who's in healthcare?

00:05:47.900 --> 00:05:50.130
Who wants to be in healthcare
in some way related?

00:05:51.390 --> 00:05:54.030
It is obviously one of the most
important things to change,

00:05:54.030 --> 00:05:55.850
it is one of the most
difficult things to change.

00:05:55.850 --> 00:05:58.880
And it varies around the world
on what the systems are.

00:05:58.880 --> 00:06:02.630
But anyways, I've always said for IBM,
it was going to be one of our moon shots.

00:06:02.630 --> 00:06:05.284
Moon shots meaning I know
how hard it is to change.

00:06:05.284 --> 00:06:10.320
It's so obvious the problems, so if it's
so obvious, why can't it get changed?

00:06:10.320 --> 00:06:11.869
And so there are many, many reasons.

00:06:11.869 --> 00:06:16.246
And anyways we started on this, and I only
tell you this story back to purpose was,

00:06:16.246 --> 00:06:19.743
I'll always remember going down
the streets of New York one day.

00:06:19.743 --> 00:06:24.540
I was with my husband and
someone, she called my name out.

00:06:24.540 --> 00:06:26.830
And I turned around and it was a client.

00:06:26.830 --> 00:06:30.150
We were working on the beginnings
of Watson in healthcare.

00:06:30.150 --> 00:06:32.620
And I can always remember,
she grabbed my shoulder and

00:06:32.620 --> 00:06:35.450
she said you know,
we will change the face of healthcare.

00:06:37.090 --> 00:06:41.735
My little part, I'm under no illusions
that I will change my little part.

00:06:41.735 --> 00:06:45.340
To me those are the moments you live for.

00:06:45.340 --> 00:06:48.200
And so that is why I stayed.

00:06:48.200 --> 00:06:49.013
Did you ask me that question though?

00:06:49.013 --> 00:06:50.837
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; I felt like telling you that story,

00:06:50.837 --> 00:06:54.010
I don't know why [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; My question is somewhere in there.

00:06:54.010 --> 00:06:56.747
&gt;&gt; I felt it was important based on
I don't know what, but go ahead.

00:06:56.747 --> 00:06:57.975
All right.
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]

00:06:57.975 --> 00:06:58.543
&gt;&gt; I think one-

00:06:58.543 --> 00:07:00.688
&gt;&gt; I don't always tell it, so that was,

00:07:00.688 --> 00:07:01.714
&gt;&gt; Thank you.

00:07:01.714 --> 00:07:04.550
&gt;&gt; You're welcome [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; More interesting answer than

00:07:04.550 --> 00:07:07.010
my question was.

00:07:07.010 --> 00:07:08.520
&gt;&gt; Why do you stay here?

00:07:08.520 --> 00:07:09.906
&gt;&gt; Sorry?
&gt;&gt; No [LAUGH]

00:07:09.906 --> 00:07:11.051
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]

00:07:11.051 --> 00:07:12.712
&gt;&gt; This is not interactive, [LAUGH]

00:07:12.712 --> 00:07:13.376
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]

00:07:13.376 --> 00:07:16.680
&gt;&gt; We'll cut that off.

00:07:16.680 --> 00:07:21.542
&gt;&gt; But I looked, you've had lots
of different jobs, already.

00:07:21.542 --> 00:07:22.400
&gt;&gt; Yeah, I've had three or
four different jobs.

00:07:22.400 --> 00:07:23.586
&gt;&gt; Several experiences, right?

00:07:23.586 --> 00:07:24.086
[LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Yeah.

00:07:24.086 --> 00:07:24.586
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]

00:07:24.586 --> 00:07:25.780
&gt;&gt; Several experiences.

00:07:25.780 --> 00:07:26.361
None of them with

00:07:26.361 --> 00:07:27.276
any purpose.
That's why I'm

00:07:27.276 --> 00:07:27.826
here.
&gt;&gt; Yeah.

00:07:27.826 --> 00:07:29.411
&gt;&gt; So that's a little easier.

00:07:29.411 --> 00:07:30.351
&gt;&gt; No, do you believe about

00:07:30.351 --> 00:07:31.757
purpose though?
Doing something

00:07:31.757 --> 00:07:32.568
on purpose?
Or

00:07:32.568 --> 00:07:33.293
just more-

00:07:33.293 --> 00:07:34.158
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]

00:07:34.158 --> 00:07:34.765
&gt;&gt; I'm just curious.

00:07:34.765 --> 00:07:35.712
&gt;&gt; [APPLAUSE]
&gt;&gt; I

00:07:35.712 --> 00:07:36.965
do now.
I do now.

00:07:36.965 --> 00:07:39.800
&gt;&gt; [APPLAUSE]
&gt;&gt; I'm not trying to put you on the spot.

00:07:39.800 --> 00:07:44.700
I am serious,
does it seem important now or maybe later?

00:07:44.700 --> 00:07:46.330
&gt;&gt; I think it always seems important.

00:07:46.330 --> 00:07:48.260
I think main purpose is something that
I think a lot of people here think

00:07:48.260 --> 00:07:48.840
about a lot.

00:07:48.840 --> 00:07:51.032
And it's one of the reasons why a lot
of people come to business school-

00:07:51.032 --> 00:07:52.282
&gt;&gt; It isn't the only reason, so

00:07:52.282 --> 00:07:54.890
don't make it, I don't mean it
to be as an altruistic point.

00:07:54.890 --> 00:07:58.960
because I think you can do multiple,
you have commercial value And

00:07:58.960 --> 00:08:00.490
purpose at the same time, right?

00:08:00.490 --> 00:08:02.807
You can learn and
have purpose at the same time.

00:08:02.807 --> 00:08:06.250
And I think those are those companies
that offer wonderful combination.

00:08:06.250 --> 00:08:09.680
And it isn't as if it's like, all you
have to every moment of every day, right?

00:08:09.680 --> 00:08:14.270
So that's all, I think you'll find
many opportunities to do both.

00:08:14.270 --> 00:08:16.820
&gt;&gt; So throughout your career
when you're faced with choices,

00:08:16.820 --> 00:08:20.150
other companies trying to lure you away
for a greater personal commercial value.

00:08:20.150 --> 00:08:23.758
Was it always that purpose you
found at IBM that kept you?

00:08:23.758 --> 00:08:27.001
&gt;&gt; I think, this purpose,
actually if I can,

00:08:27.001 --> 00:08:33.690
this purpose point I think is an important
point right now in this moment in time.

00:08:33.690 --> 00:08:36.370
Jordi and I were talking
about before I got out here.

00:08:36.370 --> 00:08:39.178
I just came back from,
it feels like it was a long time ago, but

00:08:39.178 --> 00:08:41.557
I think it was last week,
the World Economic Forum.

00:08:41.557 --> 00:08:46.300
And this year I co-chaired it, and
I co-chaired it with six other women.

00:08:46.300 --> 00:08:52.178
So highly unusual, and if you think about
it, that decision was made a year ago,

00:08:52.178 --> 00:08:55.819
actually, to have this
be women to co-chair it.

00:08:55.819 --> 00:08:59.380
But I thought hard about,
did I want to do it, and why.

00:08:59.380 --> 00:09:02.210
And it's going to be related to
the question you just asked me.

00:09:02.210 --> 00:09:05.240
So you said purpose.

00:09:05.240 --> 00:09:09.821
I actually think this is a really
interesting moment in time for technology,

00:09:09.821 --> 00:09:11.805
actually for business in total.

00:09:11.805 --> 00:09:15.454
I feel like this is this wonderful,
wonderful,

00:09:15.454 --> 00:09:20.706
we have this opportunity to solve so
many problems many of you work on.

00:09:20.706 --> 00:09:24.341
But with them come a set of challenges
that are greater than have ever been in

00:09:24.341 --> 00:09:25.052
time before.

00:09:25.052 --> 00:09:28.750
And so the reason that I
had agreed to do WEF was,

00:09:28.750 --> 00:09:32.630
this idea about responsible stewardship.

00:09:32.630 --> 00:09:37.090
Maybe there's a better word,
but I feel tech, ourselves,

00:09:37.090 --> 00:09:41.330
as well as even most companies, this
idea of what is responsible stewardship?

00:09:41.330 --> 00:09:44.402
Because there is no doubt
these technologies, and

00:09:44.402 --> 00:09:46.400
maybe we'll talk a little bit about it,
they're going to impact everybody.

00:09:46.400 --> 00:09:49.150
They're going to impact everybody's job,

00:09:49.150 --> 00:09:54.190
and they're going to happen faster than
other series have happened before.

00:09:54.190 --> 00:09:59.148
And we have this, I think, a real
issue of creating a world of have and

00:09:59.148 --> 00:10:00.867
have-nots out there.

00:10:00.867 --> 00:10:04.040
Whether it's businesses,
whether it's people, individuals.

00:10:04.040 --> 00:10:08.130
And that there is this idea that
you've got to responsibly bring

00:10:08.130 --> 00:10:09.740
these technologies into the world.

00:10:09.740 --> 00:10:12.988
So when you say to me what also
keeps me there, it is that belief.

00:10:12.988 --> 00:10:17.537
And I think this is really this moment,
maybe we will talk more about it, but

00:10:17.537 --> 00:10:21.447
this ability to usher them in
with transparency, with purpose,

00:10:21.447 --> 00:10:22.530
that word again.

00:10:23.570 --> 00:10:28.370
To also live by a set of data principles,
so the whole world doesn't end up will

00:10:28.370 --> 00:10:31.260
all value and a few hands,
I think that's wrong.

00:10:31.260 --> 00:10:35.220
And a third is then,
a real obligation of business and

00:10:35.220 --> 00:10:39.470
government and society to prepare
people to live in this world.

00:10:39.470 --> 00:10:44.463
I always think it's like transforming
a company, you can see where you are and

00:10:44.463 --> 00:10:45.758
where you want to go.

00:10:45.758 --> 00:10:47.606
You get killed in the transition, and

00:10:47.606 --> 00:10:50.490
it's always this transition
that makes the difference.

00:10:50.490 --> 00:10:55.280
And so why do I say, I believe really
strongly that we understand that

00:10:55.280 --> 00:10:58.926
stewardship, that it is
not enough to make things.

00:10:58.926 --> 00:11:02.440
You have to bring them safely into
the world, or your job is not done.

00:11:04.331 --> 00:11:05.886
&gt;&gt; There's a lot to-
&gt;&gt; You can take your notes back out, sorry

00:11:05.886 --> 00:11:07.218
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]

00:11:07.218 --> 00:11:08.820
&gt;&gt; No, there's a lot to unpack there.

00:11:08.820 --> 00:11:11.480
And I think that one of
the interesting points is,

00:11:11.480 --> 00:11:15.770
in a practical sense, how do you balance
your responsibility to your shareholders?

00:11:15.770 --> 00:11:19.080
Your need as a company to drive sort
of real technological leadership

00:11:19.080 --> 00:11:20.460
with that stewardship society.

00:11:20.460 --> 00:11:22.470
I mean,
how do you do that on a day-to-day level?

00:11:22.470 --> 00:11:26.960
&gt;&gt; What do you think, and seriously,
you must think of this in classes right?

00:11:26.960 --> 00:11:29.110
What do you think about for that?

00:11:30.370 --> 00:11:32.151
&gt;&gt; I honestly have no idea.

00:11:32.151 --> 00:11:33.700
[LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]

00:11:33.700 --> 00:11:34.947
&gt;&gt; I'm not putting you on the spot.

00:11:34.947 --> 00:11:37.590
&gt;&gt; [APPLAUSE]
&gt;&gt; I'm not putting you,

00:11:37.590 --> 00:11:41.370
because I think you actually probably know
the answer to that, to be honest with you.

00:11:41.370 --> 00:11:46.040
So to me, the answer has always been, and

00:11:46.040 --> 00:11:50.420
maybe it's because of,
you talked about our past and all.

00:11:51.690 --> 00:11:56.900
First off, if you don't define yourself
as a product, if you don't protect

00:11:56.900 --> 00:12:01.940
your past, and
then if you try to do the right thing for

00:12:01.940 --> 00:12:04.530
the long term,
I think you end up in the right place.

00:12:04.530 --> 00:12:06.930
If you do those three things.

00:12:06.930 --> 00:12:10.150
And so to me that balance,

00:12:10.150 --> 00:12:14.090
I always feel like many of us, and
again, some of you in the places you go.

00:12:16.130 --> 00:12:21.320
We are allowed to operate, because our
clients and society allow us to operate.

00:12:21.320 --> 00:12:24.606
And that build on trust, and
so in the end, society or

00:12:24.606 --> 00:12:27.580
business will decide if will trust you.

00:12:27.580 --> 00:12:31.426
And so always that in my mind,
I mean, I go back in time.

00:12:31.426 --> 00:12:35.170
Things like, and not a credit any
of my decisions, by any stretch.

00:12:35.170 --> 00:12:39.491
This goes back decades, when governments
have asked us in many countries,

00:12:39.491 --> 00:12:42.741
we were talking about today
IBM's in 170 countries.

00:12:42.741 --> 00:12:45.227
35% of our business is
from the United States, so

00:12:45.227 --> 00:12:48.090
we've got quite a lot of
experience everywhere else.

00:12:48.090 --> 00:12:51.596
How many times governments had
asked us for access to technology,

00:12:51.596 --> 00:12:53.166
back doors into technology.

00:12:53.166 --> 00:12:57.105
And we're the only tech that can say
we said no, from the beginning of time.

00:12:57.105 --> 00:12:59.995
Because our license to
operate was based on trust.

00:12:59.995 --> 00:13:04.435
And so I think this idea,
if that's the role you feel you play, and

00:13:04.435 --> 00:13:05.995
you make that decision for
the long term, and

00:13:05.995 --> 00:13:09.515
that trust will matter, you will
actually make those right decision.

00:13:09.515 --> 00:13:11.305
And I think it's true for
consumer companies.

00:13:11.305 --> 00:13:15.535
i don't think it's true for just, we're B
to B, but I think it's true for everybody.

00:13:17.090 --> 00:13:20.165
&gt;&gt; There's a lot of rising mistrust in
technology companies these days I think.

00:13:20.165 --> 00:13:25.440
And you've seen a lot of tech leaders
go to Washington publicly apologizing.

00:13:25.440 --> 00:13:29.555
How do you think the tech industry
as whole needs to rebrand itself or

00:13:29.555 --> 00:13:30.450
re-evaluate its image?

00:13:30.450 --> 00:13:32.960
Is it all part of this communication?

00:13:32.960 --> 00:13:34.800
Is it being demonstrative?

00:13:34.800 --> 00:13:35.700
How does that work?

00:13:35.700 --> 00:13:38.114
&gt;&gt; I was going to ask you what you think,
but I won't do that, okay?

00:13:38.114 --> 00:13:41.797
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Next week it'll just be me up here.

00:13:41.797 --> 00:13:44.142
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; I'll take questions from everybody and

00:13:44.142 --> 00:13:45.716
we'll see if that will work better.

00:13:45.716 --> 00:13:49.680
&gt;&gt; You're doing good, you're well
prepared, and so that's in fairness.

00:13:51.352 --> 00:13:55.290
So let's just talks a little
bit about that, right?

00:13:55.290 --> 00:13:56.610
Let me just pack in a small,

00:13:56.610 --> 00:13:59.170
I really believe this is
a moment in time for all of us.

00:13:59.170 --> 00:14:01.310
And I say this to all of my clients,
right?

00:14:01.310 --> 00:14:04.388
This is our moment for
this kind of stewardship,

00:14:04.388 --> 00:14:10.110
really principled stewardship, stewardship
about how to bring all this technology in.

00:14:10.110 --> 00:14:13.170
And so I think when you do that,
you recognized what you are and

00:14:13.170 --> 00:14:15.800
what you're not, or what you've become.

00:14:15.800 --> 00:14:19.170
And so as an example,
we'll probably talk a little bit about AI.

00:14:19.170 --> 00:14:22.850
And in AI, as an example, is one of
the many technologies, but I feel this way

00:14:22.850 --> 00:14:27.530
about others too when I say,
what do you have to do to build trust?

00:14:27.530 --> 00:14:29.430
Well, you have to bring
it to the world and

00:14:29.430 --> 00:14:34.040
be clear about its purpose,
its transparency, and its explainability.

00:14:34.040 --> 00:14:37.740
So purpose meaning, tell people,
this is to augment what man does.

00:14:37.740 --> 00:14:42.182
Honestly, I was an AI
specialist like 30 years ago.

00:14:42.182 --> 00:14:44.310
I wish it had been called
augmented intelligence,

00:14:44.310 --> 00:14:46.260
I've been saying this for a decade.

00:14:46.260 --> 00:14:49.060
Because I do really see
that we'll solve so

00:14:49.060 --> 00:14:52.658
many great problems, but
this will be a world of man and machine.

00:14:52.658 --> 00:14:54.390
And so they're going to
have to work together, and

00:14:54.390 --> 00:14:56.490
that's going to mean a lot of change.

00:14:56.490 --> 00:15:00.640
And so if you are clear that your purpose,
though, is to work with,

00:15:00.640 --> 00:15:05.540
not to replace, I'm not Pollyanna,
there are things that will be replaced.

00:15:05.540 --> 00:15:07.860
But then you'ill help people
do their job even better.

00:15:07.860 --> 00:15:11.130
And I see this, and
I can give you tons of examples of it.

00:15:11.130 --> 00:15:13.850
So purpose, then transparency,

00:15:13.850 --> 00:15:18.300
you need to, I think I need to tell
you when I'm using these technologies.

00:15:18.300 --> 00:15:20.550
And then I need to tell
you how they were trained.

00:15:20.550 --> 00:15:24.240
I need to tell you what data got used,
I need to tell you where it came from.

00:15:24.240 --> 00:15:27.888
It's one thing if you're asking
simple questions about weather, and

00:15:27.888 --> 00:15:30.947
we own the weather company,
so that's okay, no problem.

00:15:30.947 --> 00:15:34.710
You assume how weather got there or
the favorite song.

00:15:34.710 --> 00:15:37.720
But when you're asking about healthcare,
you're asking about a regulation,

00:15:37.720 --> 00:15:42.120
you're asking about the right treatment
for cancer, you care who trained it.

00:15:42.120 --> 00:15:45.570
And so then, the other thing I tell
you we've learned over the years,

00:15:45.570 --> 00:15:49.770
I mean, mistakes we have made, is you then
have to be able to explain your answers.

00:15:49.770 --> 00:15:54.059
Because if I'm a professional using AI,
do I want a black box?

00:15:54.059 --> 00:15:56.930
How many of you would like to work
with a black box, gives you an answer?

00:15:56.930 --> 00:16:00.160
You don't, you want something to say
this is why, this is the background,

00:16:00.160 --> 00:16:01.670
this is how it came up.

00:16:01.670 --> 00:16:05.140
And it took us down different paths of
how to build this kind of technology,

00:16:05.140 --> 00:16:07.050
because you have to be able to explain it.

00:16:07.050 --> 00:16:11.240
So, to me, when you said how do you
build that trust in this moment,

00:16:11.240 --> 00:16:14.980
I don't think it's about just words,
I think it is about real actions.

00:16:14.980 --> 00:16:17.770
You clear on your purpose,
you'd be clear on transparency,

00:16:17.770 --> 00:16:20.355
you'd be clear on explainability, and

00:16:20.355 --> 00:16:24.880
then you have to live by things like,
you own your data, I don't own your data.

00:16:24.880 --> 00:16:29.490
Data can freely flow, governments can't
have access, here's how I secure it.

00:16:29.490 --> 00:16:31.300
And then you help with skills.

00:16:31.300 --> 00:16:32.736
So I know that's a lot, but

00:16:32.736 --> 00:16:36.310
honestly I think that's what we
gotta do at this moment in time.

00:16:36.310 --> 00:16:40.555
This is such a special moment to
get this off on the right foot.

00:16:40.555 --> 00:16:41.634
Does that make sense to you?

00:16:41.634 --> 00:16:42.136
&gt;&gt; It does make sense to me.

00:16:42.136 --> 00:16:44.166
&gt;&gt; I could ask everyone, but
you're right next to me.

00:16:44.166 --> 00:16:47.731
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; It does make some sense to me and

00:16:47.731 --> 00:16:52.460
I guess one of the fears I might have is
someone learning a very traditional set of

00:16:52.460 --> 00:16:56.505
skills at business school is how am
I going to augment what I do with AI?

00:16:56.505 --> 00:16:58.651
Is this something that you think is
really going to be treated practical

00:16:58.651 --> 00:16:59.640
in every industry?

00:16:59.640 --> 00:17:04.409
That we're old as it is or we're
going to be replaced by the machines.

00:17:04.409 --> 00:17:07.750
&gt;&gt; How many people believe you're
replaced by a machine, seriously?

00:17:07.750 --> 00:17:10.596
I mean the way I asked it doesn't make
you want to answer my question, but.

00:17:10.596 --> 00:17:12.423
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Two brave souls are at the back.

00:17:12.423 --> 00:17:13.287
&gt;&gt; Yeah, I know, and

00:17:13.287 --> 00:17:16.261
they are counting on that I cannot
see that far at this point, so.

00:17:16.261 --> 00:17:21.766
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; We just finished another study.

00:17:21.766 --> 00:17:24.248
There have been many studies,
I read three more on my way out.

00:17:24.248 --> 00:17:28.851
Thursday, I'm going to be at a big
group of my colleagues on this topic,

00:17:28.851 --> 00:17:30.576
about the future of work.

00:17:30.576 --> 00:17:32.550
They're all over the board, right?

00:17:32.550 --> 00:17:35.810
But I believe a couple things, and then
I'll answer your question about that, so,

00:17:35.810 --> 00:17:40.630
first off, if you, and you guys study
technology better than most in the world.

00:17:42.452 --> 00:17:45.580
What's so different about I think
the moment we're in right now,

00:17:45.580 --> 00:17:49.040
is that it has the chance to have
an exponential impact, not linear.

00:17:49.040 --> 00:17:50.920
Most of the time we think
the change is linear, improvement,

00:17:50.920 --> 00:17:54.300
improvement, it goes like this,
line through it.

00:17:55.530 --> 00:18:00.021
If you go back in time Morris Law with
processors, double their 18 months,

00:18:00.021 --> 00:18:01.967
that would be more exponential.

00:18:01.967 --> 00:18:06.594
If you would go back then in time, the
social media networking later became known

00:18:06.594 --> 00:18:11.017
as metcalf's law that the value would be
the square of the nodes in a network so

00:18:11.017 --> 00:18:12.739
you have the network effect.

00:18:13.860 --> 00:18:16.601
So everybody talks endlessly
about the amount of data.

00:18:16.601 --> 00:18:17.760
It's not the amount.

00:18:17.760 --> 00:18:22.430
It's if you could really get learning to
happen exponentially off of that data,

00:18:22.430 --> 00:18:25.480
you would have another exponential
curve caused by learning.

00:18:25.480 --> 00:18:27.420
That's what I think is in front of us.

00:18:27.420 --> 00:18:32.124
So if that's true, this third kind
of big exponential curve in life,

00:18:32.124 --> 00:18:35.328
then you'd say,
does it replace everything?

00:18:35.328 --> 00:18:36.820
I don't think it replaces everything.

00:18:36.820 --> 00:18:38.640
It does change everything.

00:18:38.640 --> 00:18:39.780
So we've done all these studies.

00:18:39.780 --> 00:18:42.350
Could be 5% of jobs, 10%,
I don't think it matters.

00:18:42.350 --> 00:18:44.060
I think it's 100% change.

00:18:44.060 --> 00:18:48.775
So you prepare for that world.

00:18:48.775 --> 00:18:51.185
So two ends of a spectrum.

00:18:51.185 --> 00:18:55.582
One of the very earliest things we did
was health care, and started in oncology.

00:18:55.582 --> 00:18:57.760
Extremely difficult.

00:18:57.760 --> 00:19:01.120
And it's been an interesting road and
an interesting learning.

00:19:01.120 --> 00:19:04.920
So first off, time does matter with some
of these technologies to get started.

00:19:06.060 --> 00:19:09.410
So you may think of Watson from Jeopardy,
but much, much,

00:19:09.410 --> 00:19:11.530
much has transpired since that time.

00:19:11.530 --> 00:19:16.829
In fact, November-December,
the Oncologist which is the most trusted

00:19:16.829 --> 00:19:21.876
sort of peer-reviewed journal in
oncology put out a study on Watson.

00:19:21.876 --> 00:19:26.718
A thousand patients tumor board, and
we were able to find 30% more and 30% of

00:19:26.718 --> 00:19:31.720
the cases new things that should have been
followed than what the doctor could find.

00:19:33.210 --> 00:19:36.010
That's not an insult to doctors,
but there's a learning here, right?

00:19:36.010 --> 00:19:38.700
Because how many professionals ever want
to be told they missed something, or

00:19:38.700 --> 00:19:40.370
they got something wrong.

00:19:40.370 --> 00:19:42.060
It's very difficult.

00:19:42.060 --> 00:19:42.950
And so

00:19:42.950 --> 00:19:46.240
there's a whole change management side
to what's going to really happen here.

00:19:47.840 --> 00:19:51.290
But, I then take that same situation and

00:19:51.290 --> 00:19:55.670
we're now up to 100,000 patients in
oncology treatment in India and China.

00:19:55.670 --> 00:19:59.130
So because there, there's only one
oncologist for 1,600 patients.

00:19:59.130 --> 00:20:00.869
Here, there's one for a hundred.

00:20:00.869 --> 00:20:02.380
It's a very different world.

00:20:02.380 --> 00:20:06.910
And so that sort of idea that motherhood,
how does that saying go?

00:20:06.910 --> 00:20:11.800
Necessity is the motherhood of invention,
it's going to leap frog, I think, and

00:20:11.800 --> 00:20:16.110
driven by perhaps other personal world for
different reasons, less specialization.

00:20:16.110 --> 00:20:20.140
So there, the doctors are learning and
saying, my God,

00:20:20.140 --> 00:20:21.890
I can't do this job by myself.

00:20:21.890 --> 00:20:24.830
And I mean it isn't, it's impossible for
almost any professional, right,

00:20:24.830 --> 00:20:25.760
to do this.

00:20:25.760 --> 00:20:28.380
So I learn this lesson with that.

00:20:28.380 --> 00:20:31.255
And the technologies continued
to learn faster and faster.

00:20:31.255 --> 00:20:35.104
I remember the very first cancers
took nine to 12 months to teach,

00:20:35.104 --> 00:20:38.410
now 30 days a new cancer,
30 days a new cancer.

00:20:38.410 --> 00:20:43.403
So this idea where your job change,
I think everybody's will in professionals

00:20:43.403 --> 00:20:48.034
at one end of a spectrum and then I
said there's another end of a spectrum.

00:20:48.034 --> 00:20:50.752
Just to give you a feel for
why I say this, my own HR,

00:20:50.752 --> 00:20:55.070
I actually think, you know most people,
anybody want to be an HR professional?

00:20:56.330 --> 00:20:58.460
One, okay, see, there we go.

00:20:58.460 --> 00:21:03.338
Now it is, most people would, is it A,
a difficult job, B, there's a lot of

00:21:03.338 --> 00:21:08.308
administration that people think continue,
typically associated with it.

00:21:08.308 --> 00:21:12.648
I've got fantastic leader who has spent
four years I think making it the most AI

00:21:12.648 --> 00:21:14.708
cognitive profession in the world.

00:21:14.708 --> 00:21:17.243
Now it's changed everybody's job, but

00:21:17.243 --> 00:21:22.170
it has got everything to do with how
recruitment is done, proactive retention,

00:21:22.170 --> 00:21:25.440
compensation so
there's no bias in it as an example.

00:21:26.660 --> 00:21:29.830
Everything to do with not only answering
your questions, helping your career,

00:21:29.830 --> 00:21:33.710
what should you learn, and
that is applied everywhere along the way.

00:21:33.710 --> 00:21:37.570
And that to me is like a really normal
good example, of how life changes for

00:21:37.570 --> 00:21:38.980
all of those jobs.

00:21:38.980 --> 00:21:40.340
And they all changed.

00:21:40.340 --> 00:21:43.740
It does make a difference in that you
have people with less training and

00:21:43.740 --> 00:21:47.270
skill able to do jobs, and
it puts a premium on being an expert

00:21:47.270 --> 00:21:52.130
on the other side, because things go
very quick from beginning to expert.

00:21:52.130 --> 00:21:54.290
You almost skip right over those.

00:21:54.290 --> 00:21:57.524
Does that make, that's the kind
of world I think we'll be in.

00:21:57.524 --> 00:22:01.227
&gt;&gt; Definitely, and to go kind of away
from AI but still the future of work,

00:22:01.227 --> 00:22:04.131
something that really struck
me as I was researching for

00:22:04.131 --> 00:22:07.309
this interview was IBM's Pathways
to Technology schools.

00:22:07.309 --> 00:22:10.450
I was wondering if you could tell us a
little bit more about that and the mission

00:22:10.450 --> 00:22:13.560
and how you think future high school
as well will go through that education.

00:22:13.560 --> 00:22:17.370
&gt;&gt; How many of you, how many people
think that kids coming out of school had

00:22:17.370 --> 00:22:20.850
the right skills to be successful,
broadly speaking, not your neighbors.

00:22:22.580 --> 00:22:25.345
Okay, was it the neighbor
part that stopped you or the-

00:22:25.345 --> 00:22:25.915
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]

00:22:25.915 --> 00:22:27.915
&gt;&gt; You're like, my neighbor's kids,

00:22:27.915 --> 00:22:28.476
forget it.

00:22:28.476 --> 00:22:28.976
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; No.

00:22:30.150 --> 00:22:34.580
&gt;&gt; This is something I would like to

00:22:34.580 --> 00:22:38.590
either get some opinion when we have some
lunch afterwards and input from folks.

00:22:38.590 --> 00:22:43.380
This started with I believe back to this
point, with this moment of technology

00:22:43.380 --> 00:22:48.290
changing everything, that the skills issue
is a really big issue around the world.

00:22:48.290 --> 00:22:50.849
And I don't look at it
just in a ZIP code and

00:22:50.849 --> 00:22:53.272
in a state in one place in this country.

00:22:53.272 --> 00:22:57.450
I believe it's what's given rise to all
of the political people we've seen.

00:22:57.450 --> 00:23:00.495
There are people that don't view they
have a better future in front of them,

00:23:00.495 --> 00:23:02.500
there's a world of have and have not.

00:23:02.500 --> 00:23:07.720
And that to me is a very dangerous
place to go for any economy.

00:23:07.720 --> 00:23:12.330
And so I think the root when you
track back on all of this, is skills.

00:23:12.330 --> 00:23:15.610
And so Jordy asked me about this
thing called Pathway to Technology

00:23:15.610 --> 00:23:16.770
that we started.

00:23:16.770 --> 00:23:19.620
So this is a wonderful university, but

00:23:19.620 --> 00:23:22.710
I don't believe in the whole
globe you can say to people,

00:23:22.710 --> 00:23:27.320
you only have a future if you have a STEM
degree or you have a university degree.

00:23:27.320 --> 00:23:28.700
I believe strongly in those.

00:23:28.700 --> 00:23:30.980
I mean, that doesn't mean
I don't believe in those.

00:23:30.980 --> 00:23:35.521
But for the billions of people on this
Earth, that is not going to be the future.

00:23:35.521 --> 00:23:40.402
And so how do you therefore back to man
a machine, and can people have a really

00:23:40.402 --> 00:23:44.870
productive future, make a good income,
and have skills relevant.

00:23:44.870 --> 00:23:47.410
And so, I think there'll be
three things you have to do.

00:23:47.410 --> 00:23:49.612
One of them is, for
the youth coming through,

00:23:49.612 --> 00:23:52.800
we came up with this idea
called Pathway to Technologies.

00:23:52.800 --> 00:23:54.810
There's 100,000 kids in these schools now.

00:23:54.810 --> 00:23:56.991
So you may say,
100,000 is a drop in the bucket for

00:23:56.991 --> 00:23:58.500
billions of people on the Earth, but.

00:23:59.530 --> 00:24:02.936
It's such a reasonable start,
100,000, and is was the idea,

00:24:02.936 --> 00:24:04.370
could you take a high school?

00:24:05.700 --> 00:24:10.520
We're now in six countries,
take a high school, and

00:24:10.520 --> 00:24:12.395
some countries would call
it different things, but

00:24:12.395 --> 00:24:16.185
a community college nearby,
think of it as a six year high school.

00:24:16.185 --> 00:24:21.615
And could you come out with skills that
would be employable in a tech world?

00:24:21.615 --> 00:24:23.885
And we've proven that it's pretty true.

00:24:23.885 --> 00:24:28.625
Doubled the median income, and we picked
the worst kids in the worst schools

00:24:28.625 --> 00:24:30.625
everywhere in the world that
we could find to start with.

00:24:30.625 --> 00:24:32.875
We did not cherry pick anything.

00:24:32.875 --> 00:24:38.190
And it is heartwarming, every parent
wants for their child a better future.

00:24:38.190 --> 00:24:41.180
I don't care where you are from or
what country you are from.

00:24:41.180 --> 00:24:45.580
And every kid if given the opportunity,
so A, we help them with curriculum, B,

00:24:45.580 --> 00:24:51.362
we give them mentors, and C,
if we have jobs, we give them a chance.

00:24:51.362 --> 00:24:54.290
And hundred other companies
like ours to go in on this,

00:24:55.550 --> 00:25:01.120
everybody's happy to mentor a kid, I've
never met someone who wouldn't do it yet.

00:25:01.120 --> 00:25:05.260
And this idea, I think is becoming
a bit viral, I'm going to do

00:25:05.260 --> 00:25:09.490
all the Governor's of this country, well
I guess it's February this month already.

