00:38:26.620 --> 00:38:31.870
And what makes it so possible to really
get some traction, is you don't have

00:38:31.870 --> 00:38:35.640
to rip out the guts and rewrite everything
if you're a company to be able to use it.

00:38:35.640 --> 00:38:38.830
So the movement to it
is not that difficult.

00:38:38.830 --> 00:38:41.710
And so,
you had Doug McMillan here from Walmart.

00:38:41.710 --> 00:38:44.450
So Doug and
I started a project on food safety.

00:38:44.450 --> 00:38:48.860
You would be shocked at the number
of people who die from food, and

00:38:48.860 --> 00:38:51.190
how much food is wasted at food outbreaks.

00:38:52.360 --> 00:38:55.340
So how on earth could we
get every competitor and

00:38:55.340 --> 00:38:57.660
every food company in
the world to join on to this?

00:38:57.660 --> 00:39:01.620
They're competitors, and they're going to
share all the information about what kind

00:39:01.620 --> 00:39:05.319
of food avocados, spinach, for
the spinach breakout what happened.

00:39:05.319 --> 00:39:07.803
All that spinach wasted, and typically,

00:39:07.803 --> 00:39:11.040
we've done the tests on many things now,
but mangos.

00:39:11.040 --> 00:39:13.260
Seven days to trace
where a mango came from.

00:39:13.260 --> 00:39:15.050
We do it in two seconds now.

00:39:15.050 --> 00:39:20.450
So this idea of solving that problem,
we're doing a big joint venture with

00:39:20.450 --> 00:39:24.980
the largest shipping company in the world,
Maersk, the paperwork that accompanies

00:39:24.980 --> 00:39:31.150
a big cargo container exceeds the value
of the contents of the cargo often.

00:39:31.150 --> 00:39:34.930
So there is so much opportunity now.

00:39:34.930 --> 00:39:39.860
I said all that, it is not about bitcoin
there's something under co-blockchain,

00:39:39.860 --> 00:39:42.810
and it does matter how you implement it.

00:39:42.810 --> 00:39:45.370
And you have to have it be permissioned.

00:39:45.370 --> 00:39:47.640
Some of what's out that is opaque.

00:39:47.640 --> 00:39:49.700
I don't believe those will
be what will take off.

00:39:49.700 --> 00:39:54.150
You can't have people, that will in
fact some of, I won't use their names.

00:39:54.150 --> 00:39:56.220
Other companies that have stuff out there.

00:39:56.220 --> 00:40:02.160
It is a bit of anarchist, and you know,
it will allow crime to go on quite freely.

00:40:02.160 --> 00:40:03.370
That is not what we're talking about.

00:40:03.370 --> 00:40:07.197
We're talking about what permission
means I know who's on the network,

00:40:07.197 --> 00:40:11.813
I can tell them that professor here can
only see three things, you can see five.

00:40:11.813 --> 00:40:13.970
I know who governs this thing.

00:40:13.970 --> 00:40:15.600
I mean, who do do you think
governs the Internet, right?

00:40:15.600 --> 00:40:18.370
There is a governing body,
it's not two people.

00:40:18.370 --> 00:40:23.000
So there's a big open,
we're big fans of open source,

00:40:23.000 --> 00:40:25.980
it's the fastest growing open source,
Hyperledger is the name of it,

00:40:25.980 --> 00:40:29.340
community there's been in time
out in the Linux Foundation.

00:40:29.340 --> 00:40:32.124
And so it's governed by 200 companies now.

00:40:32.124 --> 00:40:34.630
That's a good thing,
you have to have it be immutable.

00:40:34.630 --> 00:40:36.194
Anyways, I won't give you
a lecture on blockchain.

00:40:36.194 --> 00:40:40.469
But I'm so optimistic about the things
that it can do on the positive,

00:40:40.469 --> 00:40:41.846
like food safety, and

00:40:41.846 --> 00:40:46.870
the inefficiencies it can take out to
go to better things on the other side.

00:40:46.870 --> 00:40:48.860
It's the fundamental technology.

00:40:48.860 --> 00:40:52.316
Not a comment about cryptocurrencies,
that's got a different set.

00:40:52.316 --> 00:40:55.906
But I guarantee you, whatever you do
without having governments have an ability

00:40:55.906 --> 00:40:59.195
to see some of the money transfers,
you're not going to be able to take off.

00:40:59.195 --> 00:41:01.173
So you going to go into that now?

00:41:01.173 --> 00:41:03.610
&gt;&gt; No, I'll steer clear of that.

00:41:03.610 --> 00:41:04.640
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Okay.

00:41:04.640 --> 00:41:08.511
&gt;&gt; One last question before
we turn to audience Q and A.

00:41:08.511 --> 00:41:12.030
I've been struck by the number of times
you've mentioned the healthcare industry

00:41:12.030 --> 00:41:13.337
and cancer in particular, and

00:41:13.337 --> 00:41:15.770
I know you're on the board
of Memorial Sloan Kettering.

00:41:15.770 --> 00:41:20.120
Is there a reason why this is a mission
that's very personal to you, or

00:41:20.120 --> 00:41:24.150
is it just that you see this as
the area of the biggest improvement?

00:41:24.150 --> 00:41:26.900
&gt;&gt; To me,
it is the biggest use of our GDP and

00:41:26.900 --> 00:41:29.800
it's the biggest inefficiency
with the lowest return.

00:41:29.800 --> 00:41:33.479
And it's true in a developed
country like ours, guys.

00:41:33.479 --> 00:41:37.918
But I mean, I've spent so
much time in Africa, India,

00:41:37.918 --> 00:41:42.800
China, these are unsolvable
problems in these places.

00:41:42.800 --> 00:41:46.360
These people never, ever have a chance
to get healthcare of the quality you and

00:41:46.360 --> 00:41:47.100
I experience.

00:41:47.100 --> 00:41:51.250
If any of us have cancer,
we will be seen by a cancer center.

00:41:52.830 --> 00:41:55.930
The odds of that happening outside
this country are like zero.

00:41:55.930 --> 00:41:58.440
I mean, outside a developed country.

00:41:58.440 --> 00:42:01.310
And so, that does not have to be that way.

00:42:01.310 --> 00:42:04.240
And this is to me, I mean, I guess that
they'd call it a moonshot, right, and

00:42:04.240 --> 00:42:05.820
a lot to be learned from that.

00:42:05.820 --> 00:42:10.172
But I think all of us in our companies get
a chance to work on some of those, right,

00:42:10.172 --> 00:42:11.160
so it was healthcare.

00:42:11.160 --> 00:42:15.608
But go back on the other side, I'm
the biggest fan of trying to help RegTech

00:42:15.608 --> 00:42:20.070
[LAUGH] and really basic things as well,
right, improve on the other side.

00:42:20.070 --> 00:42:24.363
But to me, us, and what we've done and
started in healthcare,

00:42:24.363 --> 00:42:27.270
it was very symbolic and important.

00:42:27.270 --> 00:42:28.959
And I think it's a really hard journey.

00:42:30.080 --> 00:42:33.730
But it is a deadly disease with
many variants and it's impossible,

00:42:33.730 --> 00:42:36.560
we started with cancer so
we started in the hardest spot,

00:42:36.560 --> 00:42:40.250
it's impossible to do without the
assistance of these kinds of technologies.

00:42:41.330 --> 00:42:42.277
&gt;&gt; Thank you so much, Jenny, and

00:42:42.277 --> 00:42:43.809
before you have time to
ask me any more questions,

00:42:43.809 --> 00:42:44.744
I think we can turn it over to-
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]

00:42:44.744 --> 00:42:46.191
&gt;&gt; The audience for some Q and A.

00:42:46.191 --> 00:42:47.976
&gt;&gt; Maybe you'd like to ask him a question,
yeah, yeah, yeah.

00:42:47.976 --> 00:42:49.564
&gt;&gt; No one can ask me questions.

00:42:49.564 --> 00:42:50.607
Turning the mic off.

00:42:50.607 --> 00:42:58.791
&gt;&gt; [APPLAUSE]

00:42:58.791 --> 00:43:00.640
&gt;&gt; Okay, anyone?

00:43:00.640 --> 00:43:03.992
&gt;&gt; Hi there, my name's Catherine,
I'm an MBA too.

00:43:03.992 --> 00:43:07.820
My question's related to your time
chairing the World Economic Forum.

00:43:07.820 --> 00:43:10.640
I think the World Economic Forum is one
of the coolest ways that private and

00:43:10.640 --> 00:43:15.180
public leaders can work together to
solve problems in a tops-down way.

00:43:15.180 --> 00:43:18.400
But as the world becomes increasingly
decentralized, how should

00:43:18.400 --> 00:43:24.440
the World Economic Forum change and
adapt to better address these problems?

00:43:24.440 --> 00:43:28.390
&gt;&gt; It is a good tops down, but
I think what happens when you leave

00:43:28.390 --> 00:43:34.010
the World Economic Forum, it becomes
a bottoms-up effort of what people can do.

00:43:34.010 --> 00:43:37.716
And I will tell you this year,
a majority of it, a lot of it,

00:43:37.716 --> 00:43:42.328
was on skills issues and how everybody
can work in partnership with that.

00:43:42.328 --> 00:43:44.008
So I think what it can do,

00:43:44.008 --> 00:43:48.090
don't try to put more into it
than it's capable of, right?

00:43:48.090 --> 00:43:51.590
So it isn't as if it's a standing body,
it is a standing body,

00:43:51.590 --> 00:43:55.959
but an organization that can actually
have rules or committees, standards.

00:43:55.959 --> 00:43:58.003
And it's more about, I think,

00:43:58.003 --> 00:44:02.870
putting standards out there that can
be followed in the rest of the world.

00:44:02.870 --> 00:44:05.220
This is a really critical time.

00:44:05.220 --> 00:44:09.642
As different countries move more inward
with each other, it's a dangerous moment.

00:44:09.642 --> 00:44:13.630
Right now, Europe is setting many,
many standards around data.

00:44:13.630 --> 00:44:16.720
Honestly, America should be in there,

00:44:16.720 --> 00:44:19.950
because they're going to end up
setting the de facto standards here.

00:44:19.950 --> 00:44:23.060
So the regulations like GDPR,

00:44:23.060 --> 00:44:26.450
global data protection, it's going to
end up being a global standard, right?

00:44:26.450 --> 00:44:29.044
And we're not at the table
as much as we should be.

00:44:29.044 --> 00:44:33.450
I know every time I'm in Europe, in
Brussels, they're dying for companies to

00:44:33.450 --> 00:44:37.750
come fill a void that maybe is seen
as a void by governments right now.

00:44:37.750 --> 00:44:40.150
And so I think what can happen,

00:44:40.150 --> 00:44:44.490
when you say what happens next,
I do think companies can help in this.

00:44:44.490 --> 00:44:45.880
In this time particularly,

00:44:45.880 --> 00:44:50.300
where you might want to call it more and
more countries look out for themselves,

00:44:50.300 --> 00:44:53.920
are more nationalistic, whatever words
you would like to put around it.

00:44:53.920 --> 00:44:58.272
I think it calls for those of us going
back, particularly around things

00:44:58.272 --> 00:45:01.681
like modernization of trade,
skills in the world, and

00:45:01.681 --> 00:45:06.556
we didn't talk much about cyber security,
right, and cyber and that impact.

00:45:06.556 --> 00:45:11.090
Because cyber is the one thing that will
stop all the, it'll take three steps

00:45:11.090 --> 00:45:15.760
forward and five steps back, right,
with cyber here, with what happens.

00:45:15.760 --> 00:45:17.930
So I think that's what we
can all go back and do.

00:45:17.930 --> 00:45:21.170
I'll always remember a meeting, years ago,

00:45:21.170 --> 00:45:24.860
that I'd had with Shimon Peres,
late in his life.

00:45:24.860 --> 00:45:28.055
And I remember a quote he had said to me,
I don't know why it's always stuck

00:45:28.055 --> 00:45:30.650
with me, but
maybe it's because I feel it's true.

00:45:30.650 --> 00:45:34.523
He said, companies can do more to
help countries than governments,

00:45:34.523 --> 00:45:36.342
because they know no boundary.

00:45:36.342 --> 00:45:45.160
And It's true,
I don't really see boundaries that way.

00:45:45.160 --> 00:45:47.546
And so
I think that's the important follow on,

00:45:47.546 --> 00:45:50.242
is what this public-private
partnership becomes.

00:45:52.783 --> 00:45:53.820
&gt;&gt; Thank you very much for your time.

00:45:53.820 --> 00:45:55.640
My name is Yaya, I'm from Saudi Arabia.

00:45:55.640 --> 00:45:57.925
I'm a joint degree candidate
here doing MBA and

00:45:57.925 --> 00:46:00.330
master's in electrical engineering.

00:46:00.330 --> 00:46:05.390
So my question to you was how can AI help
businesses that are outside the US or

00:46:05.390 --> 00:46:08.750
Europe, where the infrastructure that
allows AI to let these businesses

00:46:08.750 --> 00:46:13.790
flourish, like intelligent investors or
talent,

00:46:13.790 --> 00:46:18.050
like data scientists or something, are not
available in these countries or locations?

00:46:18.050 --> 00:46:20.200
&gt;&gt; Yeah, look, I think, first off,

00:46:20.200 --> 00:46:24.570
many of what we offer is a platform
that is available in any country.

00:46:24.570 --> 00:46:28.380
It may not run in the country, but
it's running somewhere else as a platform.

00:46:28.380 --> 00:46:33.653
And I was telling John ahead of time
about one of your colleagues here.

00:46:33.653 --> 00:46:37.110
Joshua Browder,
who is one of your colleagues.

00:46:37.110 --> 00:46:38.460
I mean, I met him only because,

00:46:38.460 --> 00:46:42.730
why would I ever meet him
other than he found Watson.

00:46:42.730 --> 00:46:45.418
And he then built a business
called DoNotPay, for

00:46:45.418 --> 00:46:49.008
people who shouldn't pay parking
tickets that they got wrongly.

00:46:49.008 --> 00:46:49.593
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; And

00:46:49.593 --> 00:46:53.973
I think he's handled 200,000 parking
tickets, those of you that know the story,

00:46:53.973 --> 00:46:54.847
$11 million.

00:46:54.847 --> 00:46:57.090
But he's working on Syrian refugees now,
and

00:46:57.090 --> 00:47:00.381
how to get them the right paperwork
to stay in different countries.

00:47:00.381 --> 00:47:03.861
And so, I think,
this is more accessible, and

00:47:03.861 --> 00:47:09.460
we do work in Saudi Arabia as well,
this is more accessible than you think.

00:47:09.460 --> 00:47:15.341
And so to me, I think this is a wonderful
technology to allow entrepreneurs,

00:47:15.341 --> 00:47:19.059
as well as big,
as well as them coming together.

00:47:19.059 --> 00:47:22.660
Actually see a lot for the coming
together between those two constituents.

00:47:22.660 --> 00:47:26.907
And if you think of it as a platform,
it's more accessible than not accessible,

00:47:26.907 --> 00:47:30.412
albeit some issues in certain countries,
but still accessible.

00:47:33.119 --> 00:47:33.870
In the back?

00:47:33.870 --> 00:47:36.680
&gt;&gt; I'm Ching Hou from the MSX program.

00:47:36.680 --> 00:47:40.100
IBM is a company, a global giant,
with a storied history.

00:47:40.100 --> 00:47:43.987
I'm wondering how you keep the IBM
family nimble, creative, and innovative,

00:47:43.987 --> 00:47:46.749
is it more more of a top-down or
bottom-up approach or?

00:47:46.749 --> 00:47:47.520
Just like to hear your views.

00:47:47.520 --> 00:47:48.425
&gt;&gt; What's your perception?

00:47:48.425 --> 00:47:50.799
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; I think it has to be both.

00:47:50.799 --> 00:47:51.395
&gt;&gt; It's okay.

00:47:51.395 --> 00:47:52.810
&gt;&gt; Yeah, I think it has to be both.

00:47:52.810 --> 00:47:55.090
Myself, I'm from the Air Force,
it's a big organization.

00:47:55.090 --> 00:47:57.640
Anytime you want to get something going,
you want to start small.

00:47:57.640 --> 00:48:00.560
You want to create space for
people, but at the same time,

00:48:00.560 --> 00:48:03.320
you need some top down direction
to say this is the vision.

00:48:03.320 --> 00:48:05.953
I'm just wondering if you have
a slightly different take on it.

00:48:05.953 --> 00:48:09.982
&gt;&gt; I do, I mean not that- this has
been one of my biggest learnings,

00:48:09.982 --> 00:48:12.710
perhaps one of my biggest
mistakes as well.

00:48:13.880 --> 00:48:16.839
So, you know, Jordy asked me this.

00:48:16.839 --> 00:48:20.159
So IBM today is an $80 billion
company in 170 countries,

00:48:20.159 --> 00:48:21.920
we talked about 46% of it new.

00:48:21.920 --> 00:48:26.240
Lots of new entrants obviously
into this technology field,

00:48:26.240 --> 00:48:29.570
and so
how do you get change to happen fast?

00:48:29.570 --> 00:48:32.415
So I'll tell you my quick- I'll try to
make a very quick story about what my

00:48:32.415 --> 00:48:33.350
learning was.

00:48:33.350 --> 00:48:37.770
So probably my first two years as CEO I
would say to the group, because it's true,

00:48:37.770 --> 00:48:41.170
the world is moving so fast,
come on, faster, faster, faster.

00:48:41.170 --> 00:48:43.920
You gotta work, I think if anyone would
have said what does she talk about they

00:48:43.920 --> 00:48:45.480
would have just said go faster.

00:48:45.480 --> 00:48:48.692
Go faster, go faster, go faster,
go faster constantly.

00:48:48.692 --> 00:48:51.050
And I thought about after
two years what did I do?

00:48:51.050 --> 00:48:54.190
I exhausted them,
that's about what I got done.

00:48:54.190 --> 00:48:58.060
Until it dawned on me that,
I have to check,

00:48:58.060 --> 00:49:01.790
it's my job and
we have to change how work is done.

00:49:01.790 --> 00:49:06.170
And so while much will ever be written
about the portfolio, and how it's changed,

00:49:06.170 --> 00:49:11.080
I think the far more,
one day revisionist history, one day when

00:49:11.080 --> 00:49:15.680
we're distanced enough we will look back
in the far more interesting story will be

00:49:15.680 --> 00:49:20.350
how work gets done and what happened
to the people and to their skills.

00:49:20.350 --> 00:49:24.730
And on the how, what we had to learn was,
we were a B2B company, so

00:49:24.730 --> 00:49:29.130
the shortcut of this is,
it meant you can be big and be fast, but

00:49:29.130 --> 00:49:31.100
you do have to re-architect work.

00:49:31.100 --> 00:49:35.070
And that means a lot of things like,
even in B2B, hey, they

00:49:35.070 --> 00:49:39.740
expect everything to be as simple as the
consumer world we all live in everyday.

00:49:39.740 --> 00:49:45.420
That means design thinking, that means we
hired 10,000 best designers from around

00:49:45.420 --> 00:49:49.820
the world, brought back that culture
that had once existed by the way in IBM.

00:49:49.820 --> 00:49:54.480
Everything is done with empathy from
the end user in, not from an engineering

00:49:54.480 --> 00:49:57.990
culture out, which is a what a lot of
tech companies are engineering out.

00:49:57.990 --> 00:49:59.810
And so it started with that.

00:49:59.810 --> 00:50:02.768
Then it started with really agile,
easy buzz word,

00:50:02.768 --> 00:50:06.678
really hard to change the way you do
work unless you truly embrace it.

00:50:06.678 --> 00:50:12.070
So we probably now 200,000 agile
experts trained, black belts.

00:50:12.070 --> 00:50:16.920
It means small, multidisciplinary,
co-located, minimum viable products.

00:50:16.920 --> 00:50:21.070
And think about minimum viable products,
I mean, when you think of big things we do

00:50:21.070 --> 00:50:25.270
like run airlines, banks,
you think of these big complex things.

00:50:25.270 --> 00:50:29.770
Going fast doesn't mean make that big,
ugly thing faster.

00:50:29.770 --> 00:50:33.200
It means no but it means start with
something little, get it right,

00:50:33.200 --> 00:50:35.500
make it bigger, make it bigger,
make it bigger.

00:50:35.500 --> 00:50:37.900
That's way different on
the way you build things.

00:50:37.900 --> 00:50:41.540
So we had a change what agile,
put that in what did it mean?

00:50:41.540 --> 00:50:43.060
Then we had a co-locate.

00:50:43.060 --> 00:50:46.760
Talk to me about a billion dollars
later in real estate, renovations and

00:50:46.760 --> 00:50:48.830
all that kind of thing around the world.

00:50:48.830 --> 00:50:53.340
And so it's been- You know, we had to
change the performance management system,

00:50:53.340 --> 00:50:55.020
we had to move in that promoter score,

00:50:55.020 --> 00:50:58.960
I mean, sorry, there's like heavy
work underneath real transformations.

00:50:58.960 --> 00:51:02.200
And that's what had to do to
happen to make you work fast.

00:51:02.200 --> 00:51:05.748
So, is that top and
that's bottom and top, it's both?

00:51:05.748 --> 00:51:10.539
But I tell you until we as leaders
help people change how they could do

00:51:10.539 --> 00:51:14.070
their work, that wasn't going to happen.

00:51:14.070 --> 00:51:17.040
And so, that to me and
then the people on skills,

00:51:17.040 --> 00:51:21.310
the skills part for
anybody changing a company,

00:51:22.550 --> 00:51:25.230
actually I'm going to talk about this
again tomorrow I think I have to do

00:51:26.620 --> 00:51:29.330
We're all going to have to change
the skills of everybody in our company.

00:51:30.380 --> 00:51:36.080
I lectured for the first Friday of every
month for four years, I taught a class.

00:51:36.080 --> 00:51:40.806
For four years, amook and it was about
what the future was, what it would be,

00:51:40.806 --> 00:51:43.375
had clients saw it over and over and over.

00:51:43.375 --> 00:51:47.438
So you paint the picture of the world that
you are building then you have to say

00:51:47.438 --> 00:51:51.182
to people, okay now I need to make it so
you can change your skills, and

00:51:51.182 --> 00:51:53.720
then I'm going to pay you for
changing them.

00:51:53.720 --> 00:51:57.280
Meaning I'm going to put compensation for
those that then go ahead and

00:51:57.280 --> 00:51:58.430
change skills.

00:51:58.430 --> 00:52:01.160
And then the third thing you have to
do after you've done that kind of

00:52:01.160 --> 00:52:04.380
reinforcement of it,
then you've got to say okay,

00:52:04.380 --> 00:52:06.930
now I'm going to make this transparent,
it's going to be obvious.

00:52:06.930 --> 00:52:10.240
We're going to post who's got that skill,
who doesn't have that skill.

00:52:10.240 --> 00:52:13.320
And that's the world of
transparency we live in, stale,

00:52:13.320 --> 00:52:16.230
hot, and you'd be surprised,
people will move.

00:52:16.230 --> 00:52:19.770
There's nothing to do with age,
this is nothing to do with age.

00:52:19.770 --> 00:52:24.990
And that's why it's changed the way I even
think about hiring and we were talking

00:52:24.990 --> 00:52:30.463
about what's cool, is it better to hire
an MBA or an- there's room for everybody.

00:52:30.463 --> 00:52:34.670
I think the most distinguishing
characteristic is your curiosity.

00:52:34.670 --> 00:52:39.510
If you are a life long
learner I would like you,

00:52:39.510 --> 00:52:42.810
because that's what is in front of us and
it doesn't matter to me what school you

00:52:42.810 --> 00:52:47.780
came out of because that's got to do with
passion, it's this life long learning.

00:52:47.780 --> 00:52:52.270
So those, the thing about the skills and
the people and then changing how

00:52:52.270 --> 00:52:57.260
work gets done, to me, that was, that will
one day be the real sense of learning,

00:52:57.260 --> 00:53:01.090
that comes out of, how do companies
re-invent themselves in this day and age?

00:53:02.790 --> 00:53:04.410
&gt;&gt; This must be the last question.

00:53:04.410 --> 00:53:05.950
&gt;&gt; Is it a good one?

00:53:05.950 --> 00:53:07.070
&gt;&gt; A lot of pressure.

00:53:07.070 --> 00:53:08.460
&gt;&gt; Or it will be the second to last?

00:53:08.460 --> 00:53:12.220
[LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; I'll try [LAUGH] So thanks so much for

00:53:12.220 --> 00:53:13.330
coming in.

00:53:13.330 --> 00:53:16.610
My name's Stephanie Young,
I'm an MBA 3, we exist.

00:53:17.660 --> 00:53:18.395
&gt;&gt; Is that a good thing?

00:53:18.395 --> 00:53:20.587
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; It depends on the MBA 3.

00:53:20.587 --> 00:53:22.240
&gt;&gt; Okay.

00:53:22.240 --> 00:53:27.360
&gt;&gt; I'm doing a joint program, and
my question for you was you talked about

00:53:27.360 --> 00:53:32.380
companies potentially crossing borders
that governments couldn't cross.

00:53:32.380 --> 00:53:34.440
And I was curious for you,
personally, with IBM,

00:53:34.440 --> 00:53:40.610
how you see IBM's role in
government in the US and globally.

00:53:40.610 --> 00:53:44.761
And conversely, flip that, and think about
what you think government needs to be

00:53:44.761 --> 00:53:49.112
doing to make it effective, to make them
effective working with companies like IBM.

00:53:49.112 --> 00:53:53.959
&gt;&gt; Yeah, look, this is a good question
because you can imagine even earlier in

00:53:53.959 --> 00:53:59.259
the year I got asked this question many,
many times and in with my whole workforce,

00:53:59.259 --> 00:54:03.870
actually to explain and
reexplain to them what is our role.

00:54:03.870 --> 00:54:09.350
And I feel, every IBM CEO has worked
with every United States President,

00:54:09.350 --> 00:54:11.050
as an example, since Woodrow Wilson.

00:54:12.110 --> 00:54:15.490
And as I said to my workforce and
my teams, I say guys,

00:54:15.490 --> 00:54:18.425
this is not about politics,
it is about policies.

00:54:18.425 --> 00:54:19.691
In fact,

00:54:19.691 --> 00:54:25.610
we are the only tech I know of that
makes no political contributions, zero.

00:54:25.610 --> 00:54:30.160
Always have a position about it,
zero, zero, zero, everywhere, zero.

00:54:30.160 --> 00:54:33.374
By the way when your answer is like
no it's easy to be consistent, no.

00:54:33.374 --> 00:54:36.520
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; And so then don't start.

00:54:36.520 --> 00:54:43.050
But it does give you a freedom
to then debate policies.

00:54:43.050 --> 00:54:45.310
And so it's policies, not politics.

00:54:45.310 --> 00:54:50.080
And so that is what I have firmly
stuck on is what my issues.

00:54:50.080 --> 00:54:53.870
I don't care in every country we
have to deal with because mind you,

00:54:53.870 --> 00:54:58.510
whether I was with last week.

00:54:58.510 --> 00:55:02.810
This week I'll do
Prime Minister Trudeau in Canada or

00:55:02.810 --> 00:55:06.927
whether it is,
She Shung Ping it doesn't matter.

00:55:06.927 --> 00:55:10.196
I've never met a country that didn't
want you to increase your workforce

00:55:10.196 --> 00:55:11.600
there, never.

00:55:11.600 --> 00:55:14.280
Labor is the currency, jobs is currency.

00:55:14.280 --> 00:55:18.770
I don't care who you are that is true for
as long as I can remember.

00:55:18.770 --> 00:55:21.870
So I think what we can do,
is each of us, so

00:55:21.870 --> 00:55:24.640
you adapt the things that
are important to you, right?

00:55:24.640 --> 00:55:28.330
So, what's important to us is education,
in every country that we work on.

00:55:28.330 --> 00:55:34.000
What's important to us is inclusion,
and diversity and this ability and why?

00:55:34.000 --> 00:55:36.660
You want it for innovation,
it's not just for

00:55:36.660 --> 00:55:39.010
an altruistic reason,
you want it for innovation.

00:55:39.010 --> 00:55:41.960
So, we're very strong
supporters of open innovation,

00:55:41.960 --> 00:55:46.570
very strong supporters of education, and
very strong supporter of free trade.

00:55:46.570 --> 00:55:51.300
I worked very hard on things like TPP
because I think they're not only good for

00:55:51.300 --> 00:55:53.720
business, they were good for
those countries, too.

00:55:53.720 --> 00:55:58.590
It held them to a level and a standard,
not to steal IP, to let data flow freely,

00:55:58.590 --> 00:56:00.500
to have wages be a certain way.

00:56:00.500 --> 00:56:03.650
There were lots of good things, and
they were modernized for the digital era,

00:56:03.650 --> 00:56:07.190
by the way, which most trade
agreements are not modernized for.

00:56:07.190 --> 00:56:11.595
So those are some of the main main issues
we have really devoted our self to.

00:56:11.595 --> 00:56:15.925
And of course, in the US,
we wanted a competitive tax system,

00:56:15.925 --> 00:56:20.775
because otherwise it allows other
countries to buy companies here, and

00:56:20.775 --> 00:56:23.707
all for those kind of reasons not for
the right reasons.

00:56:23.707 --> 00:56:27.832
So, that to me what we should do, and in
return what do you want government to do?

00:56:27.832 --> 00:56:29.977
Well you want them not to have regulate,

00:56:29.977 --> 00:56:33.537
I've never met a place where
regulation was a really great thing.

00:56:33.537 --> 00:56:39.300
But there is- you do want them to create
in every country a level playing field.

00:56:39.300 --> 00:56:42.520
So that's what I want
out of most governments.

00:56:42.520 --> 00:56:44.540
And of course the treatment of people and

00:56:44.540 --> 00:56:48.390
the focus on education but that level
playing field and that's what we work

00:56:48.390 --> 00:56:52.160
on in almost every country which is how
to be sure there's a level playing field.

00:56:52.160 --> 00:56:55.070
That people can move freely,
ideas can move freely.

00:56:55.070 --> 00:57:00.890
Right, I love that old saying, just it's
true, good ideas have no passport, right?

00:57:00.890 --> 00:57:03.710
And so that is what you want
to be able to have happen.

00:57:03.710 --> 00:57:06.950
And I'm sure it's why we fought so
hard around immigration too right.

00:57:06.950 --> 00:57:11.210
So that regardless of country,
that there should be good, and

00:57:11.210 --> 00:57:13.390
done a lot of work on immigration as well.

00:57:13.390 --> 00:57:17.730
So that would be my takeaway,
be it's always about policies,

00:57:17.730 --> 00:57:23.020
not politics and if you focus on that,
my view is you can change the world.

00:57:23.020 --> 00:57:24.087
You can change the world.

00:57:24.087 --> 00:57:27.300
&gt;&gt; Well Ginni, as an international
student struggling to get a job,

00:57:27.300 --> 00:57:30.360
I hugely appreciate that and
also want to say,

00:57:30.360 --> 00:57:33.298
believe me, you answered my questions
a lot better than I could have done.

00:57:33.298 --> 00:57:34.580
So thank you very very much for coming.

00:57:34.580 --> 00:57:35.990
Ladies and gentlemen, Ginni Rometty.

00:57:35.990 --> 00:57:37.943
&gt;&gt; [APPLAUSE]
&gt;&gt; You're a good sport.

00:57:37.943 --> 00:57:39.179
[APPLAUSE]

00:57:39.179 --> 00:57:46.180
[MUSIC]

